{"items":[{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-custom"]","description":"Send a sequence of touch events for complex gestures.
Use for: long press, drag-and-drop, custom scroll, pinch (second touch point).
For simple taps use the gesture-tap tool. For straight-line scrolling use the gesture-swipe tool. Not supported on a Mac app: use gesture-tap, gesture-drag and gesture-scroll there.
For pinch gestures use gesture-pinch. For rotation gestures use gesture-rotate.
All x/y values are fractions of the screen width/height (not pixels).
Physical iOS: a Down, any Moves, then an Up; x2/y2 adds a second finger. A plain Down then Up: same point = press-hold, other point = drag (Up delayMs = rest before the move); one Move at the Down point between them = long-press pickup (Move delayMs = hold, Up delayMs = move time). Any other train plays as timed paths, each event waiting its delayMs, and needs the app drawn in portrait.
Returns { events: number } with the total count of events dispatched. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the target device is not booted or an event type is invalid.

Example long-press at center:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.5},{"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.5,"delayMs":800}]

Example pick up a list item with a long press, then drag it up:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.6},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.6,"delayMs":800},{"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.3,"delayMs":500}]

Example smooth scroll down:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.7},
   {"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.6},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.5},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.4},
   {"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.3}]

Example pinch-to-zoom (with interpolate:10 for smoothness):
  events: [{"type":"Down","x":0.4,"y":0.5,"x2":0.6,"y2":0.5},
           {"type":"Up","x":0.2,"y":0.5,"x2":0.8,"y2":0.5}]
  interpolate: 10","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-custom"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Touch events, dispatched in order. */
  events: Array<{
      /** Touch event type */
      type: "Down" | "Move" | "Up",
      /** Touch x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      x: number,
      /** Touch y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      y: number,
      /** Second finger x, for two-finger gestures. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      x2?: number,
      /** Second finger y, for two-finger gestures. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      y2?: number,
      /** Delay before this event in milliseconds (default 16ms ≈ 60fps) */
      delayMs?: number,
    }>,
  /**
   * Number of Move events to insert between each pair of consecutive events, linearly interpolating both x,y and x2,y2 and splitting the delay evenly, for a smooth gesture. Default: no interpolation.
   */
  interpolate?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-scroll"]","description":"Send mouse-wheel events at a point to scroll content in a Chromium app or a Mac app. The anchor x/y and the deltas are fractions of the window (not pixels); x/y match gesture-tap and describe.
Use when content is below/above the fold (describe shows off-screen elements with zero height) or a list needs scrolling. Chromium and Mac apps only: on iOS/Android use gesture-swipe.
Returns { scrolled: true, timestampMs }. Fails if the Chromium CDP session is not reachable for the given device, or on a Mac app when nothing under the point scrolls.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-scroll"]({
  /** Target Chromium device id from `list-devices` (chromium-cdp-<port>), or a Mac app id. */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Anchor x. The wheel events land here; put it over the element you want to scroll. Range: 0 to 1.
   * @minimum 0 @maximum 1
   */
  x: number,
  /** Anchor y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  y: number,
  /**
   * Horizontal scroll distance as a fraction of the window width. Positive reveals content to the right; negative scrolls back left.
   */
  deltaX?: number,
  /**
   * Vertical scroll distance as a fraction of the window height (0.5 = half a window). Positive scrolls down (reveals content below), like rolling a mouse wheel toward you; negative scrolls back up.
   */
  deltaY?: number,
  /**
   * Spread the scroll over this many milliseconds in wheel-event steps (default 300) so scroll handlers fire progressively. Ignored on a Mac app.
   */
  durationMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-tap"]","description":"Press the device screen (iOS simulator or physical device, Android emulator, Mac app, or Chromium app) at a point given as fractions of the screen width and height (not pixels): a Down event followed by an Up event at the same spot. On a Mac app it is a click, and x/y are fractions of the area describe reports, not of the window.
Use when tapping a button, link, or any tappable element on the screen.
Returns { tapped: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the device backend (simulator-server / emulator / Chromium CDP) is unreachable.
Before tapping, take the coordinates from `describe` on every platform (on Chromium it walks the DOM). More information in `argent-device-interact` skill","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-tap"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id). */
  udid: string,
  /** Horizontal position: 0 = left edge, 1 = right edge. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  x: number,
  /** Vertical position: 0 = top edge, 1 = bottom edge. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  y: number,
  /**
   * Number of taps/clicks dispatched as ONE gesture inside the OS double-tap window (2 = double-tap / double-click), which two separate tap calls cannot guarantee. On physical iOS, higher counts loop single taps, which a double-tap recognizer may pair. Default 1. Range: 1 to 10.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 10
   */
  clickCount?: number,
  /**
   * Mouse button, Mac app only: "right" is a right-click, for example to open a context menu; any other device refuses it. Default "left".
   */
  button?: "left" | "right",
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-swipe"]","description":"Execute a smooth swipe / drag touch gesture between two points on the device (iOS simulator or physical device, or Android emulator). All from/to positions are fractions of screen width/height (not pixels), same as gesture-tap.
Generates interpolated Move events for a natural feel (~60fps).
Swipe up (fromY > toY) to scroll content down.
Use when you need to scroll a list, dismiss a modal, drag an element, or navigate between pages. Not supported on Chromium; use gesture-scroll there instead. Not supported on a Mac app; use gesture-scroll or gesture-drag there.
Physical iOS: an edge gesture (back-swipe) needs fromX 0 exactly; durationMs sets drag speed, not time; momentum:false only rests 300ms at the end and does not damp.
Returns { swiped: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the simulator-server / emulator backend is not reachable for the given device.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-swipe"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Start x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromX: number,
  /** Start y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromY: number,
  /** End x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toX: number,
  /** End y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toY: number,
  /**
   * Total gesture duration in milliseconds (default 300); the finger stays down this long. Below ~32ms the whole travel lands in one or two frames, which the OS flings as hard as any flick. Maximum: 10000.
   * @maximum 10000
   */
  durationMs?: number,
  /**
   * Whether the swipe releases with momentum; default true (a natural flinging swipe). false eases the finger into the end point so the OS applies little to no fling and the content stops where the finger lifts: use it when you need a deterministic scroll distance, e.g. in scroll-to-element loops. false needs durationMs >= 150 and is rejected below that; near 150 it can still land short or fling, so keep the default 300.
   */
  momentum?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-drag"]","description":"Press the left mouse button at a start point, move to an end point, and release: a desktop mouse drag in a Chromium app or a Mac app, with mouse moves interpolated at ~60fps. All positions are fractions of the window width/height (not pixels), the same space as gesture-tap and describe, except that on Chromium exactly 1.0 lands one pixel inside the window edge.
Use for slider thumbs, drag-and-drop, text selection, or draggable UI elements. Dragging never scrolls content on desktop; use gesture-scroll for lists/pages. Chromium and Mac apps only: on iOS/Android use gesture-swipe. On a Mac app the same start and end point is a long press, and a drop lands only inside the app's own windows.
Returns { dragged: true, timestampMs }. Fails if the Chromium CDP session is not reachable for the given device.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-drag"]({
  /** Target Chromium device id from `list-devices` (chromium-cdp-<port>), or a Mac app id. */
  udid: string,
  /** Press x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromX: number,
  /** Press y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromY: number,
  /** Release x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toX: number,
  /** Release y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toY: number,
  /**
   * Total drag duration in milliseconds (default 300, 800 for a long press on a Mac app); the button stays down this long. Maximum: 10000.
   * @maximum 10000
   */
  durationMs?: number,
  /**
   * Whether the drag releases with momentum; default true (a constant-speed drag). false decelerates into the release point, so an app that derives a fling from the release velocity (carousels, drag libraries) applies little to no momentum: use it when the drag must stop where it was aimed. Under ~100ms durationMs some fling survives; keep the default 300 when the fling must be fully suppressed.
   */
  momentum?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-pinch"]","description":"Execute a pinch-to-zoom gesture by moving two fingers toward or away from a center point to change the scale of on-screen content. Positions and distances are fractions of the screen (0.0–1.0, not pixels), as in gesture-tap.
startDistance > endDistance = pinch in (zoom out). startDistance < endDistance = pinch out (zoom in).
Typical values: startDistance 0.2, endDistance 0.6 for a zoom-in pinch at screen center.
Use when you need to zoom in or out on a map, image, or zoomable view. Returns { pinched: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the simulator-server / emulator backend is not reachable for the given device, or on a physical iPhone when the app is not drawn in portrait.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-pinch"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Center of the pinch, horizontal. */
  centerX: number,
  /** Center of the pinch, vertical. */
  centerY: number,
  /** Initial distance between the two fingers. */
  startDistance: number,
  /** Final distance between the two fingers. */
  endDistance: number,
  /**
   * Final horizontal center of the pinch. When set, the centroid drifts linearly from centerX to endCenterX over the gesture (e.g. to keep expanding fingers on-screen near an edge). Omit for a fixed center.
   */
  endCenterX?: number,
  /** Final vertical center of the pinch; drifts from centerY like endCenterX. Omit for a fixed center. */
  endCenterY?: number,
  /** Angle in degrees of the axis the fingers lie on: 0 = horizontal (default), 90 = vertical. */
  angle?: number,
  /** Total gesture duration in milliseconds (default 300) */
  durationMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-rotate"]","description":"Send a two-finger circular arc gesture to rotate on-screen content by a specified angle. Two fingers are placed opposite each other at a fixed radius from the center, then swept from startAngle to endAngle degrees (endAngle > startAngle = clockwise). All positions and radii are normalized 0.0–1.0 (fractions of screen width/height, not pixels), same coordinate space as gesture-tap and gesture-swipe. Typical values: radius 0.15, startAngle 0, endAngle 90 for a 90° clockwise turn.
Size the orbit with radius, or with radiusX and radiusY together (the pair overrides radius); one half of the pair alone, or none of the three, is rejected. A single radius traces a physical ellipse on a non-square screen (finger separation varies through the turn); for a physically circular orbit, pass radiusX and radiusY with radiusX·width = radiusY·height.
Unlike gesture-pinch which moves fingers linearly to zoom, this orbits fingers in an arc to change orientation.
Use when you need to rotate a map, image picker, or any rotateable UI element. Returns { rotated: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the simulator-server / emulator backend is not reachable for the given device, or on a physical iPhone when the app is not drawn in portrait.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-rotate"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Center of rotation, horizontal. */
  centerX: number,
  /** Center of rotation, vertical. */
  centerY: number,
  /** Distance from the center to each finger. */
  radius?: number,
  /** Finger distance along the horizontal axis, as a fraction of screen WIDTH. */
  radiusX?: number,
  /** Finger distance along the vertical axis, as a fraction of screen HEIGHT. */
  radiusY?: number,
  /** Starting angle in degrees (0 = right, 90 = down) */
  startAngle: number,
  /** Ending angle in degrees. */
  endAngle: number,
  /** Total gesture duration in milliseconds (default 300) */
  durationMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["run-sequence"]","description":"Execute several device interaction steps in one call.
Use when every step is known in advance and you do not need to see the screen between steps (e.g. scrolling several times, typing then pressing enter). If a step depends on the result of an earlier one (e.g. a menu item that appears only after a tap), make separate calls instead.
Steps run in order. If a step fails (an error, a tool that is not allowed or not supported on the target, or an await-ui-element that returns success=false), the sequence stops there and later steps do NOT run. Returns { completed, total, steps } with a result or an error for each step that ran, so a stopped sequence returns partial results.
One screenshot and element tree are captured after the whole sequence, not per step: call screenshot separately only for a baseline before it or to see an intermediate step. A `{{secret:...}}` in any step skips that capture, so type a secret and submit it in one sequence.
On a TV target (Apple TV / Android TV / Vega) use tv-remote for remote presses and keyboard for text.

Allowed tools, their args (? = optional) and targets. Positions and distances are 0–1 fractions of the screen (window on Chromium), not pixels; angles are in degrees, durations in ms.
  gesture-tap: x, y, clickCount?, button? "left"|"right" (macos)  [ios/android/chromium/macos]
  gesture-swipe: fromX, fromY, toX, toY, durationMs?, momentum?  [ios/android]
  gesture-scroll: x, y, deltaX?, deltaY? (signed; positive scrolls down), durationMs?  [chromium/macos]
  gesture-drag: fromX, fromY, toX, toY, durationMs?, momentum?  [chromium/macos]
  gesture-custom: events: [{ type: "Down"|"Move"|"Up", x, y, x2?, y2?, delayMs? }], interpolate?  [ios/android]
  gesture-pinch: centerX, centerY, startDistance, endDistance, endCenterX?, endCenterY?, angle?, durationMs?  [ios/android]
  gesture-rotate: centerX, centerY, startAngle, endAngle, radius (or radiusX + radiusY), durationMs?  [ios/android]
  button: button: "home"|"back"|"power"|"volumeUp"|"volumeDown"|"appSwitch"|"actionButton"  [ios/android]
  keyboard: text? or key? (one per step; key e.g. "enter"), delayMs?  [all; Apple TV / Android TV: text only]
    text may hold {{secret:<NAME>}} placeholders, resolved server-side from ARGENT_SECRET_<NAME> or an argent secrets file (over a forward-secrets link, the client's first), so a credential never enters your context
  paste: text (device clipboard + paste shortcut; only where a user would paste, e.g. an OTP, else keyboard)  [ios sim/android emu/macos]
  rotate: orientation: "Portrait"|"LandscapeLeft"|"LandscapeRight"|"PortraitUpsideDown"  [ios sim/android]
  shake: count?  [ios sim/android emu]
  fold: posture? "closed"|"half-open"|"open", or angle? 0–180, exactly one (later steps land on the new panel, whose coordinates differ)  [foldable ios sim/android emu]
  tv-remote: button (one button, or an array run as a path in one step), repeat?  [apple tv/android tv/vega]
    buttons: up/down/left/right/select/back/home/menu/playPause; rewind/fastForward/next/previous/volumeUp/volumeDown/mute on Android TV and Vega only
  await-ui-element: condition: "exists"|"visible"|"hidden"|"text", selector: { text?, identifier?, role? }, expectedText? (for "text"), textMatch?, timeoutMs?, pollIntervalMs?  [all]","signature":"tools.argent["run-sequence"]({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id), shared across all steps.
   */
  udid: string,
  /** Ordered list of interaction steps to execute sequentially. @minItems 1 */
  steps: Array<{
      /** Tool name: one of the allowed tools listed in the description. */
      tool: string,
      /** Tool arguments (excluding udid, which is injected automatically) */
      args: {
        [key: string]: unknown,
      },
      /** Wait time in ms after this step before the next (default 100) */
      delayMs?: number,
    }>,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["debugger-component-tree"]","description":"Fetch the current screen of a running React Native app as a compact tree of React components.
Each on-screen component is listed with its name, its text (or accessibilityLabel when it has no
text), [testID=…] and normalized (tap: x,y) centre in [0,1] (fractions of the screen, not pixels).
Full-screen transparent wrappers and implementation-detail components are pruned. Wrappers the size
of their parent are pruned too, so a missing name proves nothing: check with debugger-inspect-element.

Use when you need what describe does not show: a testID for a selector (on Android, describe omits
the testID of a view that has no label and is not tappable), or the component names and nesting
behind the screen before editing code. To find what to tap, use describe.

On an iOS simulator with a landscape UI (a rotated device, or an unfolded foldable), the tap
coordinates are on the screen's axes, which the gesture tools use.
Returns a compact text tree; call again after navigation. Fails if the Metro debugger is not connected.","signature":"tools.argent["debugger-component-tree"]({
  /**
   * Metro port. Omit it to use this device's port (8081 by default). Ignored for Chromium, whose CDP port is in device_id. Range: 1 to 65535.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 65535
   */
  port?: number,
  /** Device id from list-devices: the SAME id you passed to debugger-connect (iOS simulator UDID or Android serial). */
  device_id: string,
  /**
   * iOS simulator UDID from list-devices. Pass it when device_id is a logicalDeviceId (two or more devices share one Metro), so that the tap coordinates of a landscape UI are on the screen's axes. Give the UDID of the simulator that shows the app.
   */
  udid?: string,
  /**
   * When true, only components visible on screen are returned. Set to false to include all mounted components, including those scrolled off-screen, e.g. to understand the full page structure. Default: true.
   * @default true
   */
  onScreenOnly?: boolean,
  /**
   * Maximum total nodes to include. When exceeded, intermediate single-child wrapper chains are collapsed to preserve both root structure and leaf elements. Default: no limit.
   */
  maxNodes?: number,
  /**
   * When true, appends a summary of all filtered components: total fiber count and skip counts by component name and by filter. Default: false.
   * @default false
   */
  includeSkipped?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["tv-remote"]","description":"Press a TV remote / D-pad button (or a whole path of them) on a TV device: Apple TV (tvOS), Android TV (leanback), or Vega (Fire TV).
A TV is navigated with a directional remote, not touch. Use when you need to move focus or press a key on a TV, instead of gesture-tap/swipe (which do not apply on a TV).
Read the screen with `describe` before and after to see where focus landed.
Returns { pressed, count }. Fails on a target that is not a TV, and on the Apple TV simulator for rewind, fastForward, next, previous, volumeUp, volumeDown and mute (its HID stack ignores them); every other button works on all three platforms.","signature":"tools.argent["tv-remote"]({
  /** Target TV device id from `list-devices` (Apple TV, Android TV, or Vega). */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * A single TV-remote button, or a path of them run in one call. Move focus with up/down/left/right, confirm with select, go back with back/menu, exit with home; the media and volume buttons press the matching remote keys. For multi-step navigation pass an array, e.g. ["up","right","right","select"]; strongly prefer this over multiple `tv-remote` calls: the whole path runs in a single call. Max items: 64.
   */
  button: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right" | "select" | "back" | "home" | "menu" | "playPause" | "rewind" | "fastForward" | "next" | "previous" | "volumeUp" | "volumeDown" | "mute" | Array<"up" | "down" | "left" | "right" | "select" | "back" | "home" | "menu" | "playPause" | "rewind" | "fastForward" | "next" | "previous" | "volumeUp" | "volumeDown" | "mute">,
  /**
   * Repeat the whole `button` value this many times (default 1). Compact for long same-button runs, e.g. { button: "down", repeat: 12 }. Range: 1 to 50.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 50
   */
  repeat?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"}],"remaining":60,"next":{"offset":10}}

